#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3fcd243-71c6-56d2-9b1b-700f2cd11ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "69c10628-c8f2-58c6-acea-13356540156b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21814" :question/problem "The rate at which the balance in a savings account grows $B(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B$.\n\nHow many years does it take for the balance to increase by $60\\%$? Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/worked-solution "We model the balance growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nB(t) = B_0\\,e^{0.05t}\n$$\n\nwhere $B_0$ is the initial balance.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the balance has increased by $60\\%$. That is,\n\n$$\nB(t_1) = 1.6B_0\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t_{1}) &= B_{0}e^{0.05 \\cdot t_{1}} \\\\\n1.6B_{0} &= B_{0}e^{0.05t_{1}} \\\\\n1.6 &= e^{0.05t_{1}} \\\\\n\\ln (1.6) &= \\ln (e^{0.05t_{1}}) \\\\\n\\ln (1.6) &= 0.05t_{1} \\\\\nt_{1} &= (\\ln (1.6))/(0.05) \\\\\n&= t_{1} \\mid \\approx 9.40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $9$ years for the balance to increase by $60\\%$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa725153-856c-511e-8bbe-7a63286c2994" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Decay Model"}] :question/id #uuid "670e9897-4cbd-500b-ab4b-73441c16e4a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21815" :question/problem "The rate at which the amount of medicine in a person’s bloodstream decreases $M(t)$, after $t$ hours, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M$.\n\nHow many hours does it take for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount? Round your answer to the nearest hour." :question/worked-solution "We model the amount of medicine using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0\\,e^{-0.08t}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_0$ is the initial amount of medicine.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the amount of medicine is $30\\%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nM(t_1) = 0.30M_0\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.30M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-0.08t_{1}} \\\\\n0.30 &= e^{-0.08t_{1}} \\\\\n\\ln (0.30) &= \\ln (e^{-0.08t_{1}}) \\\\\n\\ln (0.30) &= -0.08t_{1} \\\\\nt_{1} &= - (\\ln (0.30))/(0.08) \\\\\n&= t_{1} \\mid \\approx 15.050\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $15$ hours for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ea69de7-f96b-5949-9aec-584cb4e2f214" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Growth or Decay Model With an Unknown Rate"}] :question/id #uuid "37b9686a-dec8-57d5-acda-9655da7de049" :question/math-academy-id "e-21816" :question/problem "During an experiment, the weight $W(t)$ of an object varies according to the differential equation $W'(t) = -r W(t)$, where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r > 0$. It is known that the object's weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. How long does it take for the object's weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nW(t) = W_0 e^{-rt}\n$$\n\nwhere $W_0$ is the weight when $t=0$.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the weight equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}W_0\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(t_{1}) &= \\frac{1}{6}W_{0}⇒W_{0}e^{-rt_{1}} = \\frac{1}{6}W_{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $t_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{0}e^{-rt_{1}} &= \\frac{1}{6}W_{0} \\\\\nW_{0}e^{-rt_{1}} &= \\frac{1}{6}W_{0} \\\\\ne^{-rt_{1}} &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\ln (e^{-rt_{1}}) &= \\ln (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\\n- rt_{1} &= -\\ln 6 \\\\\nt_{1} &= \\frac{\\ln 6}{r}(∗)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r$. We're told that the weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. Therefore, one-quarter of the weight remains, and we have\n\n$$\nW(2) = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad W_0e^{-2r} = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{0}e^{-2r} &= \\frac{1}{4}W_{0} \\\\\nW_{0}e^{-2r} &= \\frac{1}{4}W_{0} \\\\\ne^{-2r} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\ln (e^{-2r}) &= \\ln (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n- 2r &= -\\ln 4 \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 4 \\\\\nr &= \\ln 4^{1/2} \\\\\nr &= \\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(*)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{1} &= \\frac{\\ln 6}{r} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\ln 6}{\\ln 2} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $2.6$ hours for the weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aeeaf85-0cbd-58ab-91d6-93448ff54b4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Initial Value"}] :question/id #uuid "0936cc2b-c330-5f40-b87c-538cb60ccda4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21817" :question/problem "A marathon runner is participating in a race. The remaining distance $D(t)$ they have left to run $t$ hours since starting the race can be modeled by the differential equation $D'(t)= -0.8D(t)$. Two hours into the race, they have $6\\,\\text{km}$ remaining. Calculate the distance remaining after four hours." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t) = D_0\\,e^{-0.8t}\n$$\n\nwhere $D_0$ is the remaining distance when $t=0$.\n\nWe are given that the remaining distance was $6\\,\\text{km}$ after $2$ hours. So, substituting $t=2$ and $D(2)=6$ into our general solution and solving for $D_0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(2) &= D_{0}e^{-0.8 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n6 &= D_{0}e^{-1.6} \\\\\nD_{0} &= \\frac{6}{e^{-1.6}} \\\\\n&\\approx 29.718\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nD(t) = 29.718\\,e^{-0.8t}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the distance remaining after $4$ hours. So, substituting $t=4$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(4) &= 29.718e^{-0.8 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= 29.718e^{-3.2} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the remaining distance after $4$ hours is $1.2\\,\\text{km}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3fcd243-71c6-56d2-9b1b-700f2cd11ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98911908-0708-5d7b-bfbf-1d48b45d4cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$70$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178475} {:answer/id #uuid "eb413acf-c216-5944-a62d-a1e4d6c87b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178476} {:answer/id #uuid "20e9b623-fb97-53a5-8420-850122de0908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$26$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178477} {:answer/id #uuid "68483b72-d4ee-5d07-ad2a-84ac48801fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$47$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178478} {:answer/id #uuid "71e2f0e4-c5cd-5245-a6b4-ff9a41d7d385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb413acf-c216-5944-a62d-a1e4d6c87b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178476} :answer-field/id #uuid "264de395-65d7-5408-9518-83ca89eafa34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178474}] :question/id #uuid "26f43bea-64fd-52c6-be2f-1c448fb8e680" :question/math-academy-id "q-339289" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe rate at which the population of a particular country grows $P(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.02P$.\nHow many years does it take for the population to double? Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa725153-856c-511e-8bbe-7a63286c2994" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b80ca24-d20c-5d3b-8843-886f94f80157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$461$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178487} {:answer/id #uuid "bce1a42d-53d8-5882-bbf7-3b831ba76070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$328$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178488} {:answer/id #uuid "c5926881-4056-5cc9-89a0-a90a02a1ea83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$500$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178489} {:answer/id #uuid "c766890b-59d3-5481-96bc-df3fb0cb83b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1000$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178490} {:answer/id #uuid "d95e458e-b5ea-507a-bad2-7879350a0284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$625$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b80ca24-d20c-5d3b-8843-886f94f80157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$461$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178487} :answer-field/id #uuid "5254e46a-9e8b-56a6-b833-fdbde9df0baf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178486}] :question/id #uuid "81552c15-02b2-55ae-8888-41525d5709b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339321" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-0.01N$.\n\nHow many years will it take for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
